p.src:
The electromagnetic force between charges of order one in natural units has magnitude $F_{\mathrm{EM}}(r)
  \sim
  \alpha\,\frac{\hbar c}{r^2}.$ Equating it to  gives $r^2\sim\frac{G\hbar}{\alpha c^3}.$ The lecture sets the dimensionless gauge factor to order one to expose the fundamental gravitational length: $\boxed{\ell_P=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar G}{c^3}}}
  \simeq1.616\times10^{-35}\,\mathrm{m}.$ Keeping the actual $\alpha$ moves the equality with electromagnetism by an order-one logarithmic-scale factor, $r\sim\ell_P/\sqrt\alpha$. The fundamental statement is cleaner in natural units: $\begin{aligned}
  \alpha_G(E)
  &\equiv\frac{GE^2}{\hbar c^5}
  =\left(\frac{E}{E_P}\right)^2,
  \\
  E_P
  &=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar c^5}{G}}
  \simeq1.22\times10^{19}\,\mathrm{GeV}.
\end{aligned}$ Gravity becomes strong when $E$ approaches $E_P$.

